# Airline Commercial Strategist — Independent Brief

**Run:** `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
**Author lens:** 20 years in airline network planning, 10 years across the table on the airport side. My veto question on any airport standard is: *what will the carriers do in response?* Quiet-by-design lives or dies on that answer.
**Independence note:** This brief was written without reading any other Stage 1 output.

---

## Executive summary

MWAA is being asked to adopt a common quiet-by-design standard while it enters the tightest coverage window of its capital cycle and while its two dominant carriers — United at IAD and American at DCA — sit at the maximum of their leverage. United is the co-author of a $20B+ IAD transformation. American controls 53% of DCA and holds three concourses. Both signed a 15-year Use and Lease that took effect January 1, 2025, with a $9B baseline capital program (now expanded) and majority-in-interest rights on cost recovery. A standard drafted without modeling these two carriers' operational responses will either be ignored, litigated at rate-hearing, or watered down through gate-podium equipment MWAA does not directly control.

The good news for MWAA: standards are cheaper before design hardens, and Concourse E, Concourse C/D replacement, and DCA Terminal 1 are all inside the standards window. The airlines know this too. So the question isn't whether to write the standard — it's how to write it so that (a) it survives a majority-in-interest challenge, (b) it can be enforced against tenant-installed audio equipment MWAA doesn't own, and (c) it doesn't collapse the first time American misses a slot at DCA or United tries to recover an IROPS bank at IAD.

SFO's precedent is real but partial: SFO physically disabled carriers' ability to page terminal-wide and took non-routine announcements in-house at the airport's own communications desk. That works when the airport controls the audio matrix. Southwest — the most vocal carrier resistor — blocked a similar Denver initiative. That is the response function the Council must model, not around.

The airline-side recommendation: write the standard as a performance specification (STI, LEQ, zone spillover, redundant visual/mobile channels), not as an announcement-frequency cap. Bind it into the Design Manual and every new gate podium spec so it flows through consultant and tenant scopes at Concourse E, the C/D replacement, and DCA T1. Reserve announcement-volume policy for a separate governance track where airline consent is negotiated, not asserted. Anything else invites the airline reply the airport cannot afford to hear: "we'll do it our way, and we'll fight the cost recovery."

