## Key findings

1. **IAD's rate base is already leveraged, and the airlines know it.** IAD signatory CPE was reported at $12.77 for 2026 first-half and $12.88 in 2024; MWAA's adjusted debt per O&D enplanement is projected to peak near $400 in 2028 as $5.5B of new debt phases in and net coverage compresses from 1.63x to roughly 1.3x. Any standard that pushes commissioning cost into the rate base will be met at MII, not at the ribbon-cutting.

2. **United has publicly rejected the arithmetic on the IAD program.** Scott Kirby's line on the $20B+ transformation — "The $90 CPE is not correct because there's going to be more Es" — is not reassurance. It is the CEO of the anchor carrier telling investors that he expects growth to spread the fixed cost. That expectation is the airline's veto over any incremental spec that raises fixed cost without producing more Es.

3. **American Airlines' 53% share at DCA gives it MII-level leverage on any DCA-specific standard.** American operates from three concourses (C, D, E) at DCA T2. In a slot-controlled airport where a missed departure means a lost slot, not just a delayed passenger, American's operational tolerance for muted gate audio is structurally lower than at hubs with schedule slack.

4. **The relevant carrier-response benchmark is not SFO — it is Denver.** SFO removed 90+ minutes of daily announcement time by taking gate podium terminal-paging away and centralizing non-routine announcements at SFO's own comms desk. Denver tried the same policy conceptually and was reportedly blocked by Southwest, its dominant carrier. IAD and DCA have dominant carriers. Southwest is not among them, but the mechanism is.

5. **The airline audio path MWAA needs to reach is largely tenant-installed.** Gate podium PA, boarding-area speakers behind the podium, and airline club audio are typically airline-installed and airline-operated per Use and Lease specifications, with MWAA controlling the terminal-wide backbone. A quiet standard that only regulates MWAA-owned zones leaves the loudest and most contested audio uncovered. A standard that regulates tenant audio has to be written into Use and Lease amendments or Design Manual sections that survive tenant sign-off — a very different lift.

6. **IROPS is where every quiet policy breaks.** IATA's IROPS interline guidance and every carrier's operational playbook depend on rapid, low-latency, high-volume messaging when a bank collapses. United's own app now pushes gate changes and boarding progress in real time — but push notification does not clear the concourse of the 30% of passengers who won't have opted in, don't have data, or are non-English-first-language international connectors. At IAD's 36%+ international mix, that population is not a rounding error.

7. **The performance ceiling is fixed by acoustics, not by policy.** ACRP Research Report 175 recommends a minimum Speech Transmission Index of 0.45 and a signal-to-noise ratio of ~10 dB(A) for airport PA broadcasts and finds that terminals with reverberation over 2.0 seconds struggle to achieve STI 0.50 at all. Standards that specify STI, reverberation, and background LEQ get you a quieter, more intelligible terminal in one instrument. Standards that only cap announcement counts do neither, and the airlines can point that out at MII.

8. **DCA's post-January 2025 operating environment is not the moment to add discretionary announcement volume.** The DCA midair collision produced FAA restrictions, a 5.4% YoY passenger decline in 2025, and heightened irregular-operations messaging load around helicopter route changes and runway restrictions. A quiet standard drafted for a slot-constrained airport in the middle of a safety-recovery cycle needs an explicit carve-out for safety and IROPS messaging or it will be argued out of the room the first time a runway closes.

