## Evidence and analysis

### The Use and Lease framework is the real battlefield

MWAA's 15-year Airline Use and Lease Agreement took effect January 1, 2025, with a baseline $9 billion capital program (~$6.99B IAD, ~$2.39B DCA) that has since been expanded to a $20B+ IAD transformation announced July 29, 2026 [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement][Source: https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/prepare-takeoff-president-donald-j-trump-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy]. The airport-context packet notes that MII thresholds and cost-recovery categorization were not verified from primary documents. That is the exact caveat a strategist should sit inside. What we know is that any airline-recoverable cost — commissioning, tenant equipment upgrades, PA backbone replacement, DSP-based zoning matrices — has to survive whatever MII language actually says. Standards work that pretends this constraint doesn't exist gets rewritten by the Chief Financial Officer on the way to the board.

### CPE headroom is genuinely tight

IAD's 2025 signatory CPE was $12.77 with FY2025 actuals at $9.56; DCA's 2025 signatory CPE was $8.41 with FY2025 actuals at $7.49 [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/airports/iad][Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/airports/DCA][Source: https://www.mwaa.com/sites/mwaa.com/files/2025%20DCA%20Rates%20for%20Website.pdf]. IAD's number is heading up. Bond-market reporting has flagged the airport's expansion as raising questions [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions] and the Cranky Flier take on the program is bluntly skeptical [Source: https://crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/]. United's CEO said the quiet part out loud: he does not accept the arithmetic that would put IAD at $90 CPE, and he is depending on growth-driven denominator expansion to keep the rate defensible [Source: https://thepointsguy.com/news/united-dream-terminal-dulles/]. That framing tells you exactly where a quiet-by-design incremental cost will land in the airline conversation: it lands on top of a stack United already thinks is fragile.

### United at IAD is not an anchor — it is a co-author

Concourse E is a joint MWAA/United announcement: 14 new United gates, direct AeroTrain access, and a 40,000-square-foot United Club that United describes as its largest ever [Source: https://www.internationalairportreview.com/news/232900/united-and-mwaa-celebrate-500-million-concourse-e-expansion-to-enhance-passenger-experience-at-dulles/]. The $20B+ transformation was announced with the President, the DOT Secretary, and United's CEO on the podium together [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/prepare-takeoff-president-donald-j-trump-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy]. This is a partner relationship, not a landlord-tenant one. Standards written without United's operational sign-off are standards United will negotiate away during the C/D replacement design phase. Standards written with United's sign-off are the ones that ship.

### American at DCA has structural reasons to resist announcement cuts

American Airlines held 53% share at DCA at end of 2024, with more than five times the routes of the next competitor, and operates from Concourses C, D, and E [Source: https://simpleflying.com/american-airlines-washington-dc-routes-analysis-march-2025/]. DCA is a slot-controlled, high-frequency, largely domestic O&D operation. The FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 added 10 beyond-perimeter slot exemptions awarded to American, Alaska, Delta, Southwest, and United in December 2024 [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12504]. A slot missed at DCA is not just a delayed departure — it is a lost slot inside a 43-slot-per-hour cap. American has organizational memory around missed pushbacks in a way non-slot airlines don't. Every additional friction second between "close doors now" and "passenger present" is a slot-risk event. That is the operational fact underneath any American Airlines position on gate audio at DCA.

### SFO's precedent depends on a mechanism MWAA has to copy explicitly

SFO's 2018–2020 Quiet Airport program is the cited North American precedent. Reporting attributes to SFO a 90+ minute reduction in daily announcement time in the International Terminal and a 40%+ reduction in onsite noise [Source: https://viewfromthewing.com/san-francisco-airport-removed-90-minutes-of-daily-noise-travelers-say-it-changed-everything/][Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]. The methodology behind the 40% number is not primary-sourced and should be treated with the caveat the context packet already flagged. What is more useful for MWAA is the *mechanism*: SFO physically removed the boarding-area-wide paging capability from the gate podium audio systems, and non-routine announcements (final boarding, gate changes) are relayed through SFO's own communications staff on behalf of the carrier. That works only if the airport controls the PA backbone at the audio-matrix level and the tenant equipment tie-in. That is a Design Manual and Use and Lease question, not a policy memo.

### The Denver counter-example is the risk you have to model

Reporting on quiet-airport initiatives has flagged Southwest as the carrier that blocked a Denver quiet-zone program because Southwest opposed limits on non-essential gate announcements [Source: https://liveandletsfly.com/quiet-airports/]. Southwest's dominance at DEN gave it the leverage to say no. MWAA does not face Southwest as its dominant carrier, but it does face United at IAD and American at DCA. Both are structurally at least as capable of defending their operational audio as Southwest is. Any Council recommendation that treats airline compliance as a given, on the basis of SFO's success, is not modeling the carrier response function honestly.

### The performance-spec route is where airline arguments have less room to run

ACRP Research Report 175 is the standing U.S. reference on airport PA intelligibility. It recommends a minimum STI of 0.45 and a signal-to-noise ratio of ~10 dB(A) for PA broadcasts in departure lounges, and it observes that terminals with reverberation over 2.0 seconds rarely achieve STI 0.50 [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5][Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/6]. Standards written in these units — STI thresholds, LEQ ceilings, RT60 caps, zone-spillover dB drops — are hard for a carrier to argue with because they don't dictate what to say or how often. They dictate whether the message can be understood. That is a much more defensible standards conversation than "fewer announcements." It also gives MWAA a commissioning test a designer can pass or fail.

### The passenger population at IAD makes visual-first assumptions dangerous

IAD reported 10.53 million international passengers in 2025 — roughly 36%+ international share, one of the highest among major U.S. airports (per the airport-context packet). International connectors are disproportionately non-English-first-language, disproportionately less familiar with the specific carrier's app ecosystem, and disproportionately time-pressured on connections. A quiet standard that assumes push notifications and visual FIDS carry the load is a standard designed for the domestic frequent flyer at DCA, not for the Ethiopian Airlines or Emirates connector making a 55-minute hop at IAD. This is one of the strongest arguments for airport-specific implementation of a common performance framework, and against a uniform "silent airport" policy across both airports.

