## Case studies of carrier response to airport decisions

**San Francisco (SFO), 2018–2020 — carrier accommodation via mechanism control.** SFO successfully implemented gate-limited paging by removing terminal-wide broadcast capability from the airline-facing PA interface and by absorbing non-routine announcements into SFO's own communications operation [Source: https://parade.com/travel/this-major-u-s-airport-just-got-a-lot-quieter-thanks-to-a-new-policy]. Airline compliance did not require airline enthusiasm — it required the airport to control the audio matrix and the workflow. This is the model that generalizes.

**Denver (DEN) — carrier veto in action.** Southwest, DEN's dominant carrier, is reported to have blocked a proposed quiet-zone initiative because it opposed limiting non-essential gate announcements [Source: https://liveandletsfly.com/quiet-airports/]. The lesson: when the dominant carrier's operating model runs on frequent podium messaging, and the airport does not restructure the audio path around that carrier, the policy dies at the negotiating table.

**IAD Concourse E, 2026 — the airport-carrier partnership pattern.** United's 14-gate Concourse E and 40,000-sq-ft club are being delivered as a joint program, with United's CEO on the transformation announcement podium [Source: https://www.internationalairportreview.com/news/232900/united-and-mwaa-celebrate-500-million-concourse-e-expansion-to-enhance-passenger-experience-at-dulles/][Source: https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/prepare-takeoff-president-donald-j-trump-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy]. Whatever quiet-by-design provisions are written into the E specification set will effectively become the baseline for the C/D replacement, because United will not accept lower standards on its later gates than were delivered at E. This is the reverse of the "standards get argued as change orders" risk in the context packet: get it into the E spec and the standard rides the whole IAD program forward.

**DCA post-January 29, 2025 — safety messaging load is not discretionary.** The NTSB final report in February 2026 pointed to FAA and ATC failures; the FAA restricted helicopter routes and imposed runway restrictions [Source: airport-context packet, §8]. A quiet standard for DCA has to have a formal safety-and-IROPS carve-out or American will use the first IROPS event to invalidate the framework in front of the Board.

